<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · Scandinavia and the Baltic</p><h1 class="st-title">Swedish Gold Coast</h1><p class="st-meta">1650 CE – 1663 CE · capital Carolusborg</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Sweden's brief foothold in West Africa, centred on Carolusborg at Cape Coast, lost to Denmark and the Dutch within thirteen years.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Swedish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> Company was founded in 1649 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-de-geer">Louis De Geer</a>, a Dutch-born industrialist in Swedish service. Its agent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hendrik-carloff">Hendrik Carloff</a> obtained land from the local <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fante">Fante</a> rulers, and in 1653 the company built <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-coast-castle">Fort Carolusborg</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-coast">Cape Coast</a>, on the coast of modern Ghana, along with smaller lodges.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The colony was a trading enterprise, exchanging European goods for gold, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a> and enslaved people. It relied on agreements with local African rulers and on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> and Swedish crews, and it faced constant rivalry from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-west-india-company">Dutch West India Company</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danes">Danes</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1658 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hendrik-carloff">Carloff</a>, now serving <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>, seized <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-coast-castle">Carolusborg</a>. The fort changed hands several times before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> took it in 1663, ending Swedish presence in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>. As <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-coast">Cape Coast</a> Castle under the English from 1664, it became one of the main centres of the British <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">slave trade</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/swedish-gold-coast.jpg" alt="Swedish Gold Coast" width="320" height="174"><figcaption>Image: Wheatley2 · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Swedish_Empire.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1650 CE – 1663 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Carolusborg</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Hendrik Carloff</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Lutheran Christianity, Akan religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Swedish, Dutch, Fante</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/ghana">Ghana</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Swedish Guinea</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1656&amp;focus=swedish-gold-coast" aria-label="Swedish Gold Coast on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4645 1876 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Swedish Gold Coast lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5381 440h0M4995 2226h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4965 2132h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M4965 2132h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Ghana. <a href="/atlas?year=1656&amp;focus=swedish-gold-coast">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

